ganization so i i do want to  just say that i really  am committed to creating a healthy  safe workplace and that's always been my  commitment i've done that wherever i've  gone and  it will be the same at the afn okay  let's talk about your leadership and  your 100-day action plan  one of your first priorities is  recovering what is believed to be the  remains of children on the grounds of  former residential schools  have you talked to the federal  government and the various first nations  groups  on how you're going to accomplish this  yes  the prime minister did call me and he  actually called me to congratulate  me and i was very grateful for that at  the same time you know it's my  opportunity to talk to him one-on-one  so it was one of the first things i  brought up with him i said you know we  require resources to examine  every residential school across this  country a former residential school i  should say  to look for our children these are  crimes against  children they're crimes against humanity  but reparations is just  it's more than just about the amounts  that have been  given through the class action lawsuit  to  survivors of residential school you know  our whole  communities all of our communities our  whole nations  have been affected negatively by  colonialism  particularly residential schools and  there has to be reparations for that  and what that would look like really is  determined by each region  talking about their own experiences and  what they see as the path forward  are you confident though that the  federal government will come up with  funding for this  you know we just are at the tail end of  a pandemic  and the federal government pulled out i  believe over  250 billion dollars to respond to the  pandemic  and so i'm sure that they can actually  access  funds for something that's really  important not only to first nations for  healing but for  all canadians to begin to heal this part  of our  very very tragic history together chief  rosen archibald thank you for your time  thank you still ahead a canadian  airlines efforts to make a travel  comeback  eight people have been arrested in  bangladesh in connection with a deadly  factory fire  firefighters are still putting out hot  spots from thursday's massive fire  at least 52 people were killed but that  number may rise  many workers were trapped inside the  building because the main door  was illegally locked relatives of the  missing are providing dna samples to  help identify the victims  the cause of the fire is under  investigation bangladesh has a history  of deadly industrial disasters  the wife of haiti's assassinated  president is speaking out for the first  time  since his death  [Music]  in a message posted on facebook martinez  says she's alive and doing well  moise is currently being treated in  miami for injuries she suffered in the  attack  haitian police say 28 men burst into the  couple's home on wednesday  shooting and killing president jovenel  moise  so far more than a dozen men most of  them colombian  have been arrested the president's  assassination has plunged haiti  further into turmoil yesterday hundreds  of haitians gathered outside the  american embassy  pleading for a way out of the country  there were false claims on radio and  social media that the u.s would be  handing out humanitarian visas  haiti has asked the u.s to send troops  to help stabilize the country  but for now washington only plans to  send fbi  and homeland security agents to  investigate the assassination  as travel restrictions ease across the  country more canadian airlines are  returning to the skies  toronto-based porter airlines is the  latest carrier  charting its post pandemic course after  more than a year of being grounded  as ann gaviola explains the airline  industry is poised  for a comeback  porter plans to resume flying on  september 8 to select canadian  destinations including the toronto  island airport ottawa montreal and  thunder bay  adding u.s stops september 17 marking an  end to a dry spell of nearly a year and  a half the company's plans to take to  the skies have been pushed back 11 times  during the pandemic  but the ceo says he's confident about  the latest plan porter is  relaunching service september 8th which  we're thrilled about  and we'll be adding flights throughout  september and into early october  and we'll have our entire route network  of year-round destinations up and  running prior to canadian thanksgiving  there will be heightened safety measures  and that means extra training the  carrier has called back about 500  employees with plans to increase staff  as flights are added  i got sort of anxious at times to see  if and when this day would happen dave  robson has worked for porter for eight  years he's looking forward to seeing his  colleagues and passengers  in person we've had a few changes and  dates  and when i actually opened my email and  saw that  it's official i was very excited and  highly optimistic  porter secured 270 million dollars worth  of loans from the federal government  last week and  20 million of that is earmarked for  passenger refunds  now passenger refunds are being offered  as well as travel vouchers with a  premium of 25 percent  for flights that were canceled between  february 2020 and monday  and for new flights there are refunds  being offered regardless of ticket type  until late december starting this week  fully vaccinated canadians and permanent  residents who test negative for covid  don't have to quarantine for 14 days  this comes as restrictions ease across  the country  this week air canada announced the  resumption of 17 international routes  westjet is adding domestic destinations  this summer and low-cost carrier flare  airlines is adding u.s routes in the  fall  we've seen a very robust recovery in the  u.s mexico has actually got a very  robust  recovery underway as well which i think  bodes well  for a strong recovery for canadian  airlines  but we're not out of the woods yet the  border with the u.s remains closed to  non-essential travel  and although analysts predict a spike in  demand as people rush to visit friends  and family this summer  they see a dip in the fall as workers  return to the office business travel  isn't expected to bounce back until next  year  we've had an enormous response from  passengers you know that are excited to  move again we'll we'd be back at 100 on  day one  absolutely not but we do see a fairly  you know rapid recovery of traffic once  it's safe to fly again  ann gaviola global news toronto  up next cooking up culture the menu for  a top chef  and his young apprentice  an indigenous teenager is embarking on a  culinary journey  the aspiring cook has a lot to learn and  he has a lot to teach  as shelly steeds explains he wants to  serve up indigenous culture  what we're using is some tarragon right  caleb francis has been dreaming of this  moment since he was a little boy  growing up and elsie book took first  nation cooking for his family  i'm hoping to learn different cooking  techniques and different  different ways of cooking at only 14  years old  caleb has been hired by red seal chef  gene cormier  to work at his restaurant at parley  beach in sheddiac excellent  his youngest apprentice to date caleb is  honing his culinary skills  and working toward his dream to one day  open his own restaurant  where he hopes to delve more deeply into  his traditional  culinary culture when i start getting  older i want to start doing like trips  to different  reserves and finding out more about the  traditional cooking and  introduce that into my life so it can be  shared  with others it's something as dear to  his heart as cooking  i think it's important because a lot of  our language was like taken away a lot  of our culture  was like taken so i want to  introduce that back into like the world  you want to try to bunch it all together  gene looks forward to helping caleb  master his culinary techniques  and help him work toward his dreams it's  for caleb to express  himself from a culinary standpoint to  put flavors on a plate  and to showcase what represents him  nancy millier runs the youth cultural  center in elsi book took  she would like to see more employers  reach out to the indigenous community  give us opportunities for our youth to  grow  that we may not have in our own  community that we could give to them  caleb will work here for the next few  summers  developing his culinary skills and he  plans to study culinary arts after  graduation  seeing that the potential and the growth  that this kid's going to see in the next  couple years if this is what he's going  to be anchored in is going to be amazing  shelly steves global news shediac  and that is global national for this  saturday night i'm robin gill tonight  your canada  is sydney nova scotia thank you for  watching have a great night  [Music]  you
 

